      "latencyMs": 9668,
      "verdict": {
        "verdict": "misattributed",
        "earliest_node": "n9",
        "earliest_author": null,
        "earliest_date": "1913",
        "misattribution_node": "n4",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "rationale": "The earliest traceable source for the phrasing comes from a BBC article that cites the Indian Opinion newspaper in 1913 as the origin of the quote ([n9], cited_source_date: 1913). However, several independent fact‑checking and scholarly sources directly state that Gandhi did not say this. Joseph Ranseth’s analysis concludes the line is not from Gandhi ([n1]), and another site attributes the wording to Arleen Lorrance and her 1974 work “The Love Project” ([n8]). Multiple other pages note the quote is commonly but incorrectly attributed to Gandhi ([n6], [n7]). While one node erroneously lists a page date of 1869 for a Gandhi attribution ([n4]), that date is clearly erroneous (it matches Gandhi’s birth year) and does not represent an actual appearance of the saying. Because credible evidence points to an origin either later than Gandhi’s lifetime or to a different author, the saying is best considered misattributed to Gandhi."
      },
